Myth: **The Thread That Binds**

After Alara created the stars, her apprentice Kiran wished to craft something equally beautiful. He was skilled but impatient, wanting recognition for his own work.

Kiran gathered threads of starlight fallen to earth and began weaving. Unlike Alara, who listened to the Sky's needs, Kiran worked only to prove his greatness. His tapestry was technically perfect, each stitch precise, but something was missing.

When he lifted his creation toward the heavens, the Sky remained silent. The tapestry hung awkwardly, neither rising nor falling, suspended in the space between earth and sky.

"Why won't it ascend?" Kiran cried in frustration.

Alara appeared beside him. "You wove with skill but without love. Watch." She took a simple silver thread and wove it through Kiran's tapestry while singing an old song of gratitude and connection.

Suddenly, the tapestry came alive. It stretched across the horizon, creating the Milky Way's shimmering path—a bridge between earth and stars.

"True mastery," Alara explained, "comes not from perfection, but from weaving yourself into your work—your compassion, your purpose, your connection to others."

Kiran learned that day: we don't create alone. Every beautiful thing requires the threads others have spun before us.
